.TH UPTIME 1
.SH NAME
uptime \- how long the machine has been up
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uptime
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uptime
prints the time of day, how long the machine has been running since it
was last started, and how many users are logged in:
.PP
.nf
	 3:52 PM  up 2 days,  4:07,  2 users
.fi
.PP
The time up is in days, then hours and minutes; under an hour, in
minutes. The time of day is on the 12-hour clock, or on the 24-hour
clock if /etc/default/date has the line CLOCK=24 (see
.BR date (1)).
.PP
The time up comes from the clock's ticks since the machine started,
which go on whatever the date is set to, so setting the date does not
change it.
.SH FILES
.TP 20
/etc/utmp
who is logged in
.TP
/etc/default/date
CLOCK=12 or CLOCK=24
.SH SEE ALSO
date(1), who(1), xtop(1)
.SH HISTORY
An
.B uptime
command appeared in 3.0BSD.
.SH AUTHORS
This uptime was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH BUGS
4.2BSD's uptime also prints the load averages, the number of processes
waiting to run over the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes. XENIX's kernel does not
keep them, so there are none to print;
.BR xtop (1)
shows how busy the processor is.
.PP
The count of ticks starts over after nearly 7 years of running without a
restart.
